      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can see a race condition here if an interrupt is handled between get_int_mask() and interrupt_disable(), and the interrupt handler disables interrupts and does not re-enable them.\n\nAs an alternative, I could instead count nested interrupt_disable() calls, something like this:\n\nstatic int interrupt_disable_count;\n\nvoid interrupt_disable(void)\n{\n\tasm(\"cpsid i\");\n\tinterrupt_disable_count++;\n}\n\nvoid interrupt_enable(void)\n{\n\tif (interrupt_disable_count \u003e 0)\n\t\tinterrupt_disable_count--;\n\tif (interrupt_disable_count \u003d\u003d 0)\n\t\tasm(\"cpsie i\");\n}\n\nBut I worry that is too invasive and risky a change to existing behavior, may encourage nesting interrupt_disable() calls, and there may be other edge cases I haven\u0027t thought of, so I will abandon this change.",
